  "text_md": "Q1\n\n![](<1a67d1114deeff1a4ad4a6bee9422afebbb1_pg1_pg1_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\n# April–June 2016\n\n![](<1a67d1114deeff1a4ad4a6bee9422afebbb1_pg1_pg1_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\nASAP\n\nwww.asaproject.org-uk\n\n# ASAP quarterly monitoring report\n\n# DUTY SCHEME\n\n# TOTAL NUMBER OF APPELLANTS ASSISTED\n\nDuty Scheme Numbers of people assisted by ASAP In Q1 we helped 178 appellants. Including dependants the total number of people helped was 269. We assisted 75% of appellants who had oral hearing (excluding cases that we could not have helped with, even if we had been able to). 1  \n\nDuty scheme: total number of appellants assisted in Q1\n\n![](<1a67d1114deeff1a4ad4a6bee9422afebbb1_pg1_pg1_images/imageFile3.png>)\n\n140\n\n117\n\n120\n\n106\n\n106\n\n100\n\n90\n\n88\n\n83\n\n81\n\n80\n\n73\n\n68\n\n57 59\n\n57\n\n56\n\n60\n\n# NOTES 1\n\n40\n\n40\n\nThese are cases that are withdrawn or adjourned prior to the hearing, cases that are designated by the Tribunal as being “ASAP barred” (the Tribunal prohibits us from assisting when an appellant is represented by a law firm unless they received written consent from the firm) or cases where the client failed to attend or refused our assistance. 2 There is some overlap in\n\n20\n\nSep\n\nAug\n\nMay\n\nFeb\n\nApr\n\nJun\n\nJul\n\nOct\n\nNov\n\nDec\n\nJan\n\nMar\n\nTotal number of appeals listed 2016/17\n\n2015/16\n\n2016/17\n\n# APPEAL OUTCOMES\n\nthese categories as some cases concerned more than one subject (for example they may have related to both destitution and further submissions). 3 Judicial review.\n\n<table>\n  <tr>\n    <th> </th>\n    <th>Q1</th>\n    <th>Q2</th>\n    <th>Q3</th>\n    <th>Q4</th>\n    <th>2016/17</th>\n    <th>2015/16</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>% Allowed</td>\n    <td>57</td>\n    <td> </td>\n    <td> </td>\n    <td> </td>\n    <td>57</td>\n    <td>55</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>% Remitted</td>\n    <td>15</td>\n    <td> </td>\n    <td> </td>\n    <td> </td>\n    <td>15</td>\n    <td>16</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>% Dismissed</td>\n    <td>25</td>\n    <td> </td>\n    <td> </td>\n    <td> </td>\n    <td>25</td>\n    <td>25</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>% Other</td>\n    <td>3</td>\n    <td> </td>\n    <td> </td>\n    <td> </td>\n    <td>3</td>\n    <td> </td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n\n\n3 Judicial review.\n\nThis figure is adjusted to take into consideration clients whose appeals were withdrawn or who didn’t attend their hearings.\n\nThe vast majority of cases that were remitted were appeals against discontinuation decisions. A decision to remit in these circumstances means that the appellant will keep their support so we consider this to be a successful outcome. By adding this proportion to our allowed appeals, our ‘success rate’ was 71%.\n\n# APPEAL SUBJECT\n\n78% of cases related to Section 4 (s4) support; 22% of cases related to Section 95 (s95) support.\n\nl appeals were marginally more likely to be dismissed (27% of s95 cases were dismissed compared with 24% of s4 cases). We believe this difference was due to the fact that the majority of s95 appeals related to complex destitution appeals. TOP 5 ISSUES IN Q1: 2\n\n# TOP 5 ISSUES IN Q1:2\n\n1. s4 regulation 3(2)(e) – further submissions cases (72 cases = 46% caseload):   64% allowed, 19% dismissed, 17% remitted\n\n64% allowed, 19% dismissed, 17% remitted\n\n87% of appeals were allowed where further submissions had been rejected and the JR? was at pre-permission stage\n\nWe won 56% of appeals where the client was preparing further submissions but these had not been handed to the Home Office further 16% of these cases were also remitted. yet.\n\n  70% allowed, 23% dismissed, 2% remitted\n\n  s4 and s95 destitution cases had different outcomes; 81% of s4 destitution appeals were allowed compared with 55% of s95. This reflects our experience that s95 cases have been getting increasingly complex and difficult to prepare. 3. s4 regulation 3(2)(a): reasonable steps cases (8 cases = 5.1% caseload):\n\n3.s4 regulation 3(2)(a): reasonable steps cases (8 cases = 5.1% caseload):\n\n62.5% allowed, 37.5% dismissed\n\n![](<1a67d1114deeff1a4ad4a6bee9422afebbb1_pg1_pg1_images/imageFile4.png>)\n\n",
